{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Neuron Activation Space.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Neuron Activation Space.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"neuron activation space neuron activation space","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Neuron Activation Space.md","fileName":"Neuron Activation Space.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Neuron Activation Space","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 02:18\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Machine Learning and Neural Networks]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Machine Learning and Neural Networks","Model Input Space","Model Weight Space","Decision Boundary","Activation Function","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Neuron Activation Space.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Neuron Activation Space.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"neuron activation space neuron activation space","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Neuron Activation Space.md","fileName":"Neuron Activation Space.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Neuron Activation Space","heading":"Neuron Activation Space","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Kelleher distinguishes an activation space from the raw [[Model Input Space|input space]] and the [[Model Weight Space|parameter space]]. In his geometric example, weighted inputs are represented in the activation space where a threshold decision is applied. The resulting [[Decision Boundary]] can also be projected back to the original input coordinates.\n\nThe distinction is useful because an input case, the weights used to transform it, and the transformed value on which a unit acts are different objects. For a neuron, a weighted sum is followed by an [[Activation Function]] that maps it to the neuron's output activation. The activation-space view helps explain how a boundary produced aft